# Image Classification Project

## Requirements

- Windows, macOS, or Linux
- Python 3.10 (the pinned TensorFlow version does not support every newer Python version)
- Git

## Setup on another laptop

Clone the repository and open a terminal in the project folder:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Kanojkumar2001/2-1-sdc-soft-mod3-handson1.git
cd 2-1-sdc-soft-mod3-handson1
python --version
python -m venv .venv
```

Use Python 3.10.x. If `python --version` is not 3.10.x, install Python 3.10 and create the environment with `py -3.10 -m venv .venv` on Windows. Using a fresh virtual environment is important; it prevents packages from another project from causing import errors.

Activate the virtual environment.

Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Windows Command Prompt:

```bat
.venv\Scripts\activate.bat
```

macOS/Linux:

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
```

Install dependencies:

```bash
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Run the application

```bash
python -m streamlit run app.py
```

Then open the URL printed by Streamlit, normally `http://localhost:8501`.

The image quality analysis works without a trained CNN checkpoint. To enable CNN training and prediction, run:

```bash
python scripts/train_cnn.py
```

To train the hybrid model:

```bash
python scripts/train_hybrid.py
```

The command-line entry point is also available:

```bash
python main.py --mode predict --image data/test/00_001.png --model hybrid
```

The commands can be launched from any current directory when you run them from the project folder. Dataset paths in `config/config.yaml` are resolved from the project directory, so they do not depend on a user's laptop-specific absolute path. Keep the dataset in `data/train`, `data/validation`, and `data/test`; TensorFlow datasets may use class folders or files named like `class_image.jpg`.

If TensorFlow reports a dependency import error, recreate the environment and reinstall from `requirements.txt` rather than installing packages into the system Python:

```powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force .venv
py -3.10 -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```
